<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petro Shelest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1996">1996</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petro Shelest was first secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from 1963 to 1972. While loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, he defended Ukrainian economic interests and tolerated some promotion of Ukrainian language and culture. He was removed in 1972, accused of nationalist leanings, and replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-shcherbytsky">Volodymyr Shcherbytsky</a>, who led a renewed campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shelest was born on 14 February 1908 into a peasant family in the village of Andriivka in the Kharkiv Governorate, the seventh child of the household. As a boy he herded livestock on a state farm, worked as a hired labourer, a postman and a railway loader, and later as a locksmith at the Kharkiv Locomotive Repair Factory. He joined the Komsomol in October 1923 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> in 1928, and graduated from the Mariupol Metallurgical Institute in 1935.</p><p>After a year in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> he moved to party work in 1937, as thousands of members were swept away in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>. From 1943 to 1954 he managed large factories in Leningrad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, and from 1954 he rose through the Kyiv city and regional party committees, becoming First Secretary of the Kyiv regional committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As First Secretary from 1963 Shelest tried to run <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> with some independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, pressing for economic management at the republic's level, and construction of the four nuclear plants at Chernobyl began in his time. He became a full member of the Presidium when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> fell in November 1964. Historians disagree on how far he personally backed the Sixtiers, and he was criticised by Viacheslav Chornovil for the arrests of 1965–1966. Party membership in Ukraine reached 2.5 million by 1971.</p><p>In 1968 he took a leading part in the Soviet response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>, attending every meeting with the Czechoslovak leaders alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a>. He insulted František Kriegel as a Galician Jew, and on 3 August received from Vasiľ Biľak the letter inviting Soviet troops that served as a pretext for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion on 20 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Removed in May 1972, Shelest spent eleven months as a Deputy Premier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> before being demoted again; officially his health was blamed. In April 1973 a Ukrainian press article condemned his book O Ukraine, Our Soviet Land of 1970 for ideological errors likely to encourage nationalism. He managed an aircraft design bureau near Moscow from 1973 to 1985, blamed his fall on intrigues by Brezhnev and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-shcherbytsky">Volodymyr Shcherbytsky</a>, and returned to lecture in Ukraine after the Soviet collapse. He died in Moscow on 22 January 1996.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petro Shelest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Stalin, Aleksey Kirichenko became in 1953 the first ethnic Ukrainian to lead the party since the 1920s; <b>Petro Shelest</b>, removed in 1972, was followed by Volodymyr Shcherbytsky's Russification.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Andriivka, Balakliia Raion, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.53,36.62&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Andriivka, Balakliia Raion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5392 438 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Petro Shelest"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5842 708h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5842 708h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.53,36.62&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
